  "body": "Generated MCP tools drop explicit `None` arguments through `exclude_none=True`, including parameters whose advertised input schema allows null. Conversely, the generated Pydantic model can accept `None` for optional fields whose advertised schema disallows it.\n\nPreserve caller presence with `exclude_unset=True`, then filter object members against the original input schema. This works through local references, nested objects and arrays of objects in both fresh and persistent session modes. Array positions, zero, false and explicitly supplied defaults are preserved.\n\nFor structured unions, try branches that accept the original input before attempting to omit nulls to fit another branch. A valid explicit null then survives either branch order, including when a separate common constraint requires filtering. An unresolved reference alone retains the transformer's permissive fallback, but sibling type/enum/const and composition constraints still apply regardless of keyword order. Validation uses an empty reference registry and does not retrieve remote schemas.\n\nIntegrated current `main` (`14cf8eb`), retaining #313's dictionary-schema handling. `jsonschema` and `referencing`, already transitive MCP dependencies, are declared directly; no locked package versions change.\n\n### Payload compatibility\n\nOmitted optional arguments, including non-null schema defaults, are no longer sent. Servers can apply their own defaults; callers that need a default in the request must supply it explicitly. Explicit `None` for a nonnullable optional object member is omitted, including nested members. Genuinely nullable members retain explicit null. This behavior is documented in `docs/examples/mcp_agent.md`.\n\n### Validation\n\nWindows / Python 3.13, current locked dependencies:\n\n- All **117 serializer/argument-presence tests pass**, including the original 79 cases. The 38 additional review cases cover union branch order, arrays, common constraints, unresolved local/remote references, sibling keyword order and composition restrictions. Against the previous PR serializer, 18 fail and 20 are passing controls; all pass with this follow-up.\n- Generated-tool tests assert the actual `session.call_tool` arguments in both session modes. Resolvable advertised schemas are also checked with JSON Schema validation.\n- Full core suite: **951 passed, 3 credential-gated MiniMax integration tests skipped**; 99% total statement coverage.\n- Repository-wide Black and Flake8, plus `git diff --check`, pass.\n- MCP transports and sessions are mocked; no live LLM or external MCP integration run is claimed.\n",
